(Master's finding: the first cut of `Free` only taught `emit_free_buffer`
 to recognise the shared released header - `Resize` (and anything else
 that can hand a buffer pointer to `_realloc_buffer`/`_reallocate_buffer`)
 did not know about it. `Resize`d, the released header fell into the
 alloc+copy fallback, which attempted `munmap` on the header's own
 `.data` address (harmless today only because 24 bytes there is not
 page-aligned) and then silently resurrected the variable as a fresh,
 live, writable buffer - `append` afterward really landed in memory, and
 a second `Free` doubly-munmapped a real block without ever flagging.
 The fix moved the shared header into coreasm as `_released_buffer_header`
 and taught `_free_buffer`, `_realloc_buffer`, and `_reallocate_buffer`
 to refuse it by address before touching memory, so every caller - not
 just `Free`'s own codegen - is safe. `Resize` on a freed buffer must
 refuse and flag, leave the buffer empty, and a later `append` and a
 later `Free` must both still see it as freed.)

a buffer called line is "hello".
Free line.

Resize line to 100.
On error Print "resize flagged".
print "length after resize {line's length}".

append "abc" to line.
On error Print "append flagged".
print "text [{line as text}]".

Free line.
On error Print "second free flagged".
